(   2. Hardware Root of Trust \(RoT\) and Secure Elements:) Tj
T*
(   - Hardware Root of Trust: An inherently trusted, immutable hardware block etched directly onto) Tj
T*
(   silicon that serves as the foundation for all platform security operations.) Tj
T*
(   - Physical Unclonable Functions \(PUF\): Leverages microscopic, uncontrollable silicon) Tj
T*
(   manufacturing variations \(e.g., SRAM startup state patterns\) to generate a unique, non-volatile) Tj
T*
(   cryptographic 'silicon fingerprint' that is never stored in memory and cannot be cloned even by) Tj
T*
(   reverse-engineering.) Tj
T*
(   - Secure Key Storage: Dedicated tamper-resistant internal flash memory with physical) Tj
T*
(   zeroization mechanisms if physical decapping or voltage tampering is detected.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Hardware Security Module \(HSM\) & Secure Boot Lifecycle:) Tj
T*
(   - Cryptographic Accelerators: Dedicated hardware execution engines performing high-speed) Tj
T*
(   AES-128/256 symmetric encryption, RSA/ECC asymmetric digital signature verification, and) Tj
T*
(   SHA-256/3 cryptographic hashing with zero CPU overhead.) Tj
T*
(   - The Secure Boot Chain of Trust: \(1\) On power-up, execution starts strictly from immutable) Tj
T*
(   Boot ROM; \(2\) Boot ROM verifies the cryptographic digital signature of the first-stage) Tj
T*
(   bootloader using the public key permanently burned into on-chip One-Time-Programmable \(OTP\)) Tj
T*
(   eFuses; \(3\) If the signature matches, execution passes to the bootloader, which in turn) Tj
T*
(   cryptographically verifies the operating system kernel before execution; \(4\) If any signature) Tj
T*
(   check fails \(indicating tampered or corrupted code\), the system executes a safe lockdown,) Tj
T*
(   preventing malicious code from executing.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
